  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 250 PM Wednesday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry weather with up and down temperatures \nexpected through Friday. \n\nMorning low level thickness values increase nearly 20m over a 24-\nhour period ending at 12Z Thursday. This moderating air mass ahead \nof an approaching cold front will result in warm weather on Thursday \nwith highs mainly in the mid 80s which is about 6 to 12 degrees \nabove average. \n\nA moisture starved cold front moves through the area on Thursday \nafternoon and Thursday night marking the leading edge of a cooler \nand drier air mass that moves into the region late Thursday night \nand on Friday. Highs on Friday will be 8 to 10 degrees cooler than \non Thursday and range in the mid and upper 70s which is about 0 to \n4 degrees above average.  \n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...The likelihood of unsettled weather and significant \nrain associated with Isaias with periods of rain and possible \nthunderstorms continues to increase for late Saturday and especially \nSaturday night into Sunday. \n\nTropical Storm Isaias is forecast to quickly strengthen into a \nhurricane on Thursday, move ashore on the northern Gulf Coast on \nFriday night near MS/AL/western FL with the circulation center \ntracking north into the western TN Valley on Saturday night into \nSunday. Meanwhile a cooler and drier air mass will invade the \nCarolinas on Friday into Saturday with highs in the 70s and \ndewpoints on Friday into at least the first part of Saturday mainly \nin the upper 40s to lower 50s. A coastal front will develop on the \nwestern periphery of this developing CAD airmass over the interior \nand try to advance inland on Saturday and then surge inland on \nSunday. \n\nThe timing of rainfall is problematic, especially with the onset \ntime. While some spotty rain and showers are even possible late \nFriday night into early Saturday, mainly across the southern and \nsoutheastern areas, more general/areawide rainfall chances will \nincrease from south to north on Saturday and especially Saturday \nnight. The main surge of widespread rain driven by isentropic lift \nin the 295 to 305K layer lifts north across the area from 6pm \nSaturday to 9am Sunday. A bit of a lull is expected early Sunday \nfollowed by a north-south band or two of showers and possible storms \nthat move east across the area. These bands will provide the only \nreal severe weather potential if enough instability can materialize \nand work with a moderate to high shear environment. Timing and other \ndetails will need to be resolved but this will be in the family of a \nlow end high shear low CAPE environment. Scattered showers, likely \nof the low impact variety, are likely to continue into the first \npart of Sunday night before quickly ending with improving conditions \nfrom west to east by Monday morning. \n\nAverage forecast rainfall amounts from WPC have been adjusted upward \nacross central NC and should range between 1 and 2 inches with \nlocally higher amounts across southeastern areas where instability \nwill be the greatest and perhaps western areas where the isentropic \nlift may be the strongest. -Blaes",
